<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="d3489dbf">&lt;p&gt;
    The technical consultants on a project team should hold meetings and review the programs and technical specifications
    that have been produced and unit tested. Functional consultants should be involved to advise on appropriate test cases,
    creation of test data and interpretation of unit test results. If necessary, code and documents should be revised and
    re-tested.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="ae3be529"></attribute>
<attribute name="usageGuidance" nodeId="4860fe65"></attribute>
<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="33c2df9">&lt;p&gt;
    Conduct a peer review meeting in a format like the following:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The meeting leader opens with a short discussion of the goal of the meeting and lays out any ground rules. The
        developer explains their code, what it is supposed to accomplish, what requirements it contributes to, and what
        documentation it affects. Each participant (usually fellow development team members) raises questions, comments,
        and suggestions identified in the preparation phase. The participants may reference a code review checklist to make
        sure their review is thorough and complete. The developer addresses any areas of concern and explains the logic,
        problems, and choices that resulted in this code. Lastly, the team checks to make sure there is no unused code.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        If there are defects or alternatives found in the meeting, document them and address them appropriately
        afterwards.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
</Element>
</reference>
